---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Window from "../../components/Window.astro";
import dashboard from "../../assets/img/lantern-dashboard.webp";
import phone from "../../assets/img/lantern-dashboard-phone.webp";
---

<Base
  title="Lantern Analytics — case study — Ayach Youssef"
  description="Lantern Analytics: cookieless web analytics where the schema itself has no column for an IP, a cookie or a fingerprint, plus an AWS Terraform module tested with a mocked provider. Built with AI coding agents under my direction."
>
  <!-- ============ TITLE ============ -->
  <section class="study-head container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Case study — side project, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="display">Lantern Analytics</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Web analytics that cannot track a person, because the database has nowhere
      to put the information it would need. A tracking snippet under two
      kilobytes, an ingest API, a server-rendered dashboard, and an AWS
      Terraform module to host it on a single small instance.
    </p>

    <div class="meta-band">
      <div class="meta-cell">
        <span class="eyebrow">Role</span>
        <p>Direction and review; built with AI coding agents</p>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-cell">
        <span class="eyebrow">Timeline</span>
        <p>November 2026</p>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-cell">
        <span class="eyebrow">Stack</span>
        <p>Hono &middot; SQLite &middot; Terraform (AWS) &middot; Docker</p>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-cell">
        <span class="eyebrow">Status</span>
        <p>
          Source on <a
            href="https://github.com/YoussefAyach01/lantern-analytics"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener">GitHub</a
          >. 33 tests. Terraform validated, never applied.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ THE PROBLEM ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
        <h2 class="display">
          &ldquo;Privacy-friendly&rdquo; usually means &ldquo;we hash it&rdquo;
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          Most lightweight analytics tools still write something identifying to
          disk so they can tell a returning visitor from a new one: a hashed
          cookie, a stored IP, a long-lived fingerprint. The promise is that the
          hash is not personal data. The honest version of that promise is
          shorter: it depends.
        </p>
        <p>
          The brief for this one was stricter. Count visits and pageviews, show
          top pages and referrers, run on a small self-hosted box, and make the
          privacy claim something a reader can check in the schema rather than
          take on trust.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal" style="margin-top: var(--space-12)">
        <Window
          title="lantern-analytics — dashboard"
          src={dashboard}
          alt="The Lantern Analytics dashboard for a site called Atlas Coffee Roasters: two totals, 833 visits and 1360 pageviews, a bar chart of the last 14 days, and a top-pages table listing /, /menu, /blog/how-we-roast and others with visits and pageviews"
          caption="Two weeks of seeded traffic for a fictional roaster. Totals, a daily chart, top pages, top referrers. No client-side framework, no cookies."
          eager
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ DESIGN ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">The design</p>
        <h2 class="display">Four properties the code enforces</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="bento">
        <article class="card reveal" style="--index: 0">
          <h3>No column for it</h3>
          <p>
            The <code>events</code> table has a path, a referrer host, a device class,
            a timestamp and one flag, <code>is_new_visit</code>. There is no IP
            column, no cookie id, no hash. A privacy test serialises a real
            inserted row and asserts the IP and the user agent never appear in
            it.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card reveal" style="--index: 1">
          <h3>Dedup that forgets at midnight</h3>
          <p>
            Telling a second pageview from a new visit uses an in-memory set of
            SHA-256 hashes of a random daily salt, the site, the IP and the user
            agent. At UTC midnight the salt is regenerated and the set is thrown
            away. Tests prove the same visitor hashes differently across the
            boundary and never rotates mid-day.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card reveal" style="--index: 2">
          <h3>Rollups you can re-run</h3>
          <p>
            Daily stats are recomputed per site and day by deleting that
            day&rsquo;s rows and re-deriving them from raw events in one
            statement. Running the rollup once, twice or five times produces
            identical totals, and a test does exactly that with a random count.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card reveal" style="--index: 3">
          <h3>No oracle for guessing</h3>
          <p>
            The collect endpoint answers an unknown site token with the same
            empty 204 it gives a valid one, so a token cannot be probed. Reads
            always need the owning API key; one key&rsquo;s dashboard is not
            reachable with another&rsquo;s, and a test tries.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ INFRA ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Infrastructure</p>
        <h2 class="display">AWS Terraform, tested without an AWS account</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          The hosting is one EC2 instance, an Elastic IP, a security group and
          an optional Route 53 record, written as a reusable module with an
          example and the same shape as my earlier DigitalOcean module.
          <code>allowed_ssh_cidrs</code> has no default, so port 22 open to the world
          cannot happen by omission; the AMI and subnet have no defaults either, because
          a module should not guess your region.
        </p>
        <p>
          It has never been applied, because no AWS account exists for this
          project yet, and the README says so in the first paragraph. What has
          run: <code>terraform fmt</code>, <code>validate</code> on the module and
          the example, and <code>terraform test</code> against a mocked AWS provider,
          which plans the instance, the IP and the security group, confirms the DNS
          record only appears when a zone id and domain are supplied, and confirms
          the SSH rule always carries the caller&rsquo;s CIDR and never <code
            >0.0.0.0/0</code
          >. CI runs all of it on every push with no credentials.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="shots-pair reveal" style="margin-top: var(--space-12)">
        <Window
          title="dashboard at 375px"
          src={phone}
          alt="The same dashboard on a phone-width screen: totals stacked, the 14-day chart, top pages and referrers tables, and the embed snippet with the note 'No cookies, no IP storage, no persistent fingerprint'"
          caption="Mobile-first by construction: the page is plain HTML and one stylesheet."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ HOW IT WAS BUILT ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it was built</p>
        <h2 class="display">With AI coding agents, and one bug they shipped</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          The code and tests were written by AI coding agents under my
          direction, in the same working pattern as <a
            href="/projects/site-kit/">Site Kit</a
          >: I set the brief and the rules, reviewed what came back, and decided
          what counted as done. The brief was explicit that the privacy property
          had to be enforced by schema and tests, not by a sentence in the
          README, and that is the part of the output I checked hardest.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first time I put real-looking traffic through it and opened the
          dashboard, the 14-day chart was an empty box. Every number was right;
          the bars were set with inline <code>style</code> attributes, and the page
          ships a strict content-security policy that silently drops those. The agents
          had written the policy and the chart separately and never loaded the page
          in a browser. The fix is heights as classes, plus a test that the dashboard
          HTML contains no
          <code>style=</code> at all. <a href="/log/lantern-privacy-by-schema/"
            >The Ship Log entry</a
          > has the rest.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ LIMITS ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Honest limits</p>
        <h2 class="display">What this is not</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          It is a single SQLite file on a single instance, with in-memory rate
          limiters that reset on restart: fine for a handful of sites, not a
          multi-region design. There is no signup or billing; API keys are
          minted by an admin call. Device detection is a user-agent regex. And
          &ldquo;no cookies, no IP storage&rdquo; is a true, tested property of
          the code, which is not the same as a lawyer saying no consent banner
          is needed in your jurisdiction.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="featured-actions reveal" style="margin-top: var(--space-12)">
        <a href="/log/lantern-privacy-by-schema/" class="btn btn-primary"
          >Read the Ship Log entry</a
        >
        <a
          href="https://github.com/YoussefAyach01/lantern-analytics"
          class="btn"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Source on GitHub</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
